        // Working with a public adjuster
        ['q' => 'What does a public adjuster do?', 'a' => '<p>A public adjuster is licensed to represent the policyholder, not the insurance company, on a property insurance claim. That usually means inspecting the damage, reviewing the policy, preparing a detailed estimate and supporting documentation, presenting the claim to the insurer, and negotiating on your behalf. In Texas, public adjusters are licensed and regulated by the Texas Department of Insurance under Insurance Code Chapter 4102. Read more in <a href="/what-is-a-public-claims-adjuster/">what is a public claims adjuster?</a></p>'],
        ['q' => 'Do I need a public adjuster for my claim?', 'a' => '<p>Not always. Small, simple claims are often resolved fairly without outside help. A Dallas public adjuster tends to be most useful when the loss is large or complex, the claim was denied or underpaid, the insurer\'s estimate leaves out parts of the damage, or the process is eating up more time than you have. See <a href="/knowing-when-to-call-a-public-adjuster/">knowing when to call a public adjuster</a>.</p>'],
        ['q' => 'How much does a public adjuster cost in Texas?', 'a' => '<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. If the insurer pays or commits in writing to pay policy limits within 72 hours after the loss is reported, a percentage fee is not allowed. Our <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> costs nothing and carries no obligation.</p>'],
